  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms, some of which may be strong\nor even severe, are becoming increasingly more likely late\nMonday afternoon and evening with a fast moving, mid to upper \nlevel disturbance arriving to the area. Models, specifically \nthe latest NAM3k, have backed off a bit on a morning complex of \nstorms across the coalfields, but this solution may still be \nprobable depending on a weakening MCS that models show \ntraversing the Ohio River Valley west to east very early Monday.\n\nSufficient and robust CAPE (2,000-4,000 J/Kg) is modeled to \nbuild Monday afternoon depending on what happens early Monday \nand if the atmosphere can recover. Latest guidance shows \nconvection firing across Ohio between 4 and 6 PM on Monday \nfollowing a northwest to southeast motion, depending on the \nlocation of a cold front. The main risk will be damaging winds, \nbut the threat for a tornado or two across Ohio or western West \nVirginia is present with the ample CAPE, 40-60 knots of \neffective shear, 250MB jet streak, and 100-300 m^2/s^-2 of \nhelicity. The window for greatest severe potential currently \nlooks to be between 4 PM and 10 PM on Monday.\n\nThe threat for severe weather continues overnight Monday into\nTuesday morning, but ultimately a transition to flash flooding\nwill be the main concern as moisture transport from the\nsouthwest creates PWATs between 1.75\" and 2.50\". Despite a more\nprogressive typology of these showers and thunderstorms, the\nlisted factors will lead to very efficient rainfall rates. \n\nCurrent guidance has between 0.50\" and 1.50\" of rainfall \nfalling across most of the area Monday night through Tuesday. \nAmounts around or above 2 inches could be picked up across \nportions of the southern coalfields and along the mountains, \nthis includes some areas that already received devastating \nflooding not even a week ago.\n\nThis set up does not seem to warrant the same alarm bells for\ncatastrophic flooding as the previous event, but heavy rainfall\nis looking to be of concern and will require full attention the\nnext few model runs. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nConditions quiet down some on Wednesday as high pressure remains\nstiff and unwavering across the southern CONUS. Our weather will\nbe much drier for the remainder of the week, outside of chances\nfor isolated showers and thunderstorms during peak heating each\nafternoon. Models show rounds of mid to upper level disturbances\nthat will cross each day providing more confidence in diurnal\nchances for convection. However, severe weather is not likely\ndue to lack of strong enough support aloft with these weak waves\nof vorticity. \n\nKeeping an eye on the weekend as another very strong system\nlooks to take aim at the forecast area, but details could change\ndrastically between now and then.",
